# Host tests

Run the portable entrance rules test from the repository root:

```sh
gcc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Iinclude \
  components/entrance/core.c test/test_entrance.c -o /tmp/test_entrance
/tmp/test_entrance
```

Run Trevor's portable room/protocol tests:

```sh
gcc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -Iinclude \
  test/test_trevor.c components/trevor/core.c -o /tmp/test_trevor
/tmp/test_trevor
```

`test/test_runtime.c` exercises the FreeRTOS adapter with Windows thread and
GPIO stubs. Run the full suite on Windows with PowerShell and a C compiler:

```powershell
./scripts/test-trevor.ps1 -Compiler 'C:\path\to\tcc.exe'
```

These host tests check firmware logic; they do not verify ESP32 electrical
behavior, Wokwi hardware interactions, physical multi-board timing, or reboot
recovery. For current end-to-end evidence, see
[`../docs/COURSEWORK_COMPLETION_PLAN.md`](../docs/COURSEWORK_COMPLETION_PLAN.md).

Regression tests for keypad masking, encoded HTTP schedule forms, strict CLI
times, idle/partial/overlong serial input, concurrent schedule commits, bounded
record printing and NVS/SD roster recovery:

```powershell
$env:CC = 'C:\path\to\tcc.exe'
$env:TEST_BUILD = "$env:TEMP\cocis-tests"
python .\test\test_regressions.py
```

The regression harness compiles the actual affected C function bodies. It uses
host substitutes for HTTP requests, NVS, task input and synchronization; those
checks do not claim an end-to-end Wokwi run or physical power-loss verification.
